title: Correctness firewalls enable safe integration of synthesized database components
source: hn
url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05173
date: '2026-11-11'
authors:
- Mohammad Javad Amiri
- Carsten Binnig
- Andreas Kipf
- Tim Kraska
- Ryan Marcus
- Johannes Wehrstein
- Chenyuan Wu
- Geoffrey X. Yu
- Zijie Zhao
why_read: Read this to understand how architectural firewalls can safely harness LLM-generated code in data systems without sacrificing correctness guarantees. You will learn design patterns across advisory, guarded, and certified components that yield significant performance gains.
hn_id: '50456920'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50456920
image: /infographics/01-hn-50456920.jpg
---

Coding agents can generate bespoke, instance-optimized database components with surprising speed. However, placing unverified LLM output into storage engines and query execution paths introduces catastrophic correctness risks.

Researchers from MIT, TUM, and Brown have introduced correctness firewalls: architectural boundaries where synthesized code influences only performance, never correctness. The paper establishes three distinct patterns: advisory firewalls for heuristics like cardinality estimation, guarded firewalls for binary searches on sorted arrays, and certified firewalls for compression.

In benchmarks, agent-synthesized guarded search kernels outperformed learned indexes by up to 3x, while specialized compression routines beat Zstd by 1.5x without risking data integrity.

Separating optimization heuristics from correctness verification is the key blueprint for integrating autonomous agents into mission-critical systems.

