Most vector search setups in retrieval-augmented generation treat query vectors and document keys symmetrically. Traditional approximate nearest neighbor systems like HNSW or product quantization index keys entirely in isolation, implicitly assuming queries follow the exact same distribution. In production RAG, that assumption breaks down: queries are short, informal, and drawn from a consistent real-world user base, while stored keys are long and curated.

Apple Machine Learning Research tackles this asymmetry by learning support functions directly over the query distribution. Instead of exhaustive comparisons or query-agnostic partitioning, this amortized Maximum Inner Product Search framework trains lightweight support structures tailored to recurring question patterns.

Optimizing vector search for production means exploiting domain-specific query distributions rather than relying entirely on generic geometric indexing structures.

