Booting Apple Silicon macOS on generic x86-64 hardware requires bridging two radically distinct architectures: the ARM64 ISA and Apple proprietary Metal graphics pipeline.

Stela approaches this challenge through a custom UEFI boot layer. It implements an ahead-of-time and just-in-time compiler that covers more than 3,000 AArch64 instruction encodings, mapping guest ARM registers directly to host x86-64 registers with aggressive translation caching.

The graphics layer is equally ambitious. Rather than emulating an entire display pipeline in software, its Metal driver ingests Apple Intermediate Representation (AIR) and compiles it down directly to Mesa NIR for modern NVIDIA GPUs, supporting both Metal3 and Apple7 GPU families.

While still experimental, this project offers an exceptional masterclass in bare-metal systems programming, runtime binary translation, and compiler backend design.

