---

A critical vulnerability has been uncovered in Apache HTTP Server's HTTP/2 implementation. Designated as CVE-2026-119618, the flaw allows concurrent execution against shared resources in mod_http2's assign_headers() function, triggering heap corruption and potential remote code execution.

The vulnerability impacts Apache HTTP Server versions 2.4.55 through 2.4.70, as well as standalone mod_h2 builds from 2.0.8 through 2.0.42. Because HTTP/2 multiplexes multiple streams over a single TCP connection, synchronization bugs inside header processing pipelines expose core edge proxies directly to malicious payloads.

Notably, this bug was discovered and reported by Anthropic's automated OSS security scanner. If you run Apache in front of production microservices or APIs with HTTP/2 enabled, patching to 2.4.71 is mandatory.

Concurrency bugs in memory-unsafe protocols remain one of the most stubborn risks in foundational web infrastructure.

