Most developers evaluate coding agents by model capability and output accuracy, ignoring what the client runner is doing to their local machine. A rigorous benchmark comparing agents like Claude Code, Codex, and open source alternatives reveals surprising system overhead. Memory footprints across seven runners diverge by up to 74x, spanning from 5.8 MiB in Zig up to 430 MiB in Node and Bun.

The most dramatic bottleneck is not token processing or subprocess management. The terminal user interface itself frequently consumes more CPU cycles than the underlying agent execution. Under high token streaming rates, redrawing UI elements into stdout can consume between 35 and 48 percent of an entire CPU core just to paint diffs and spinner frames.

Decoupling the execution harness from the display layer yields instant efficiency wins. Running an agent headlessly cuts CPU time down to a fraction of interactive sessions while running identical model prompts and tool calls. If you are building CLI agents or developer tooling, treat terminal rendering as a performance-critical hot loop rather than free terminal output.

Great local agent harnesses must be engineered as real-time systems, because unoptimized terminal drawing can quietly choke your machine.

