Modern analytical databases are rarely bound by raw CPU arithmetic. The actual bottleneck is memory bandwidth. When an execution engine spends all its cycles waiting for data to travel from RAM over the memory bus, squeezing more throughput requires rethinking memory layout entirely.

In recent STAC-M3 tick analytics benchmarks, the database l compressed 2.8 terabytes of raw data down into 1.25 terabytes of RAM on a single virtual machine while setting performance records. The core mechanism is vector lane packing, which stores arrays in narrowed integer representations alongside compact block headers that describe value ranges.

Crucially, the engine does not decompress these vectors into intermediate memory buffers before executing operations. It computes aggregations and filters directly over the encoded representations. By keeping working sets compact, execution pipelines maximize CPU cache residency and minimize bus traffic.

Reducing byte movement across the memory bus is often more effective than algorithmic optimization.

